      "text": "To find the coordinates of the point on the line that passes through \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\(x\\)-coordinate of 4, we start by determining the parametric equations of the line. The direction vector of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two given points:\n\n\\[\n\\vec{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t(3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the parametric equations:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 - t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the point is 4. Substituting \\(x = 4\\) into the parametric equation for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\(t\\), we subtract 2 from both sides:\n\\[\n2 = 3t\n\\]\nThen, we divide both sides by 3:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\) into the parametric equation for \\(z\\) to find the \\(z\\)-coordinate:\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the \\(z\\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
