      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line passing through \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we start by finding the parametric equations of the line.\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector\nFirst, we determine the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line. The direction vector can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two given points:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Parametric equations of the line\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (x_0, y_0, z_0) + t \\mathbf{d}\n\\]\nwhere \\((x_0, y_0, z_0)\\) is a point on the line. We can use the point \\((2, 2, 1)\\):\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Substitute the given \\( x \\)-coordinate\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. Using the parametric equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\( t \\)\nSolve the equation for \\( t \\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t \\implies 4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Find the corresponding \\( z \\)-coordinate\nNow, substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = 1 + (-1)t - 3t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - t - 3t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 4t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 4 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\frac{3}{3} - \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nz = -\\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{5}{3}}\n\\]",
